They just explain the law.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:07.660
They just explain the law.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:12.180
And Mitchell has spent years documenting this very specific divergence in how the

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.780
United States funds healthcare.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:17.380
A divergence between the 50 states and the territories.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:18.860
Precisely.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:25.700
So if you live in any of the 50 states or Washington, D.C., Medicaid operates as an open-ended entitlement

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:26.700
program.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:27.700
Okay.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:28.700
Open-ended.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.540
Let's break down what that actually means mechanically for the listener.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.540
Sure.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:34.740
It means the federal government agrees to match a percentage of whatever the state

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.620
spends on eligible healthcare.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:37.620
Right.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:42.140
That matching rate is called the Federal Medical Assistance Percentage, or FMAP.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:43.140
Okay.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.020
How is that percentage actually calculated for like a regular state?

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.620
It's tied directly to per capita income.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:54.900
It is a mathematical formula that's designed to ensure poorer states get more help.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:55.900
Makes sense.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:56.900
Right.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:00.020
If a state has a lower per capita income compared to the national average, the federal

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:03.300
government shoulders a larger burden of their Medicaid costs.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.300
So there's a range.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.300
Yeah.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:09.620
By law, that federal match can range from a floor of 50% for wealthier states up

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.340
to a statutory maximum of 83% for the poorest states.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.340
Okay.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.540
So if a state hits a severe economic downturn, their per capita income drops and the federal

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:22.460
formula just automatically adjusts to give them a higher matching percentage to help them

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:23.460
weather the storm.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:24.460
Exactly.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:25.460
It's dynamic.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.260
But the most crucial word I used a moment ago was open-ended.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.260
Right.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:33.580
If a state experiences a massive public health crisis, say an epidemic or a natural

00:04:33.580 --> 00:04:38.620
disaster, and suddenly tens of thousands of newly eligible people need care?

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.140
The federal government doesn't cap its contribution.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:42.140
Right.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:48.260
It pays its calculated FMAP percentage for every single eligible dollar spent, whether

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:52.660
the total bill for the state is $100 million or $10 billion.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.940
You know, I like to think of this like an open tab at a restaurant.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:56.940
Oh, like that.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:57.940
Yeah.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:59.900
If you have an open tab and you've agreed with your wealthy uncle that he will

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:04.540
cover, say, 60% of the bill, it doesn't matter how many cousins show up to the

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:05.540
dinner.

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:06.540
Right.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:07.540
Right.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.780
If you continue, your uncle just keeps paying his 60%, even if the final check is

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:12.980
astronomical.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:15.020
That is the perfect way to visualize it.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:19.020
The funding scales automatically with the population's need.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:23.860
But this is where Mitchell's research points out this massive structural fork in

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:24.860
the road.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:25.860
Here we go.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.860
Puerto Rico and the other U.S. territories, they do not operate under that state

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:29.860
formula.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:30.860
Yeah.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:34.100
They're governed by a completely different, very obscure section in the Social

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.260
Security Act.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:36.260
Section 1108G.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:37.260
Section 1108G.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:40.400
And what does Section 1108G do to that open tab?

