00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:41.980
for doctors.

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:42.620
OK.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:46.220
Initially, they had to prove they were paying providers at least 70%

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:49.340
of what Medicare Part B would pay for the exact and same services

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:50.780
on the mainland.

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:54.540
Later, that requirement was bumped up to 75%.

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.220
I want to pause on this.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:59.380
Why is the federal government micromanaging

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:04.060
how much a local territory pays its pediatricians or cardiologists?

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:06.100
Because of the medical brain chain.

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:08.820
Congress realized that if they just dumped money into the system

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:11.220
without mandating provider rates,

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:15.540
the local government might use the funds to cover structural deficits

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:17.860
rather than paying doctors a competitive wage.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:18.500
Right.

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:20.420
And if you were a doctor in Puerto Rico

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:23.820
carrying hundreds of thousands of dollars in medical school debt

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.660
and you're being reimbursed at a fraction of what you'd make in Texas.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:27.460
You'd buy a plane ticket.

00:13:27.460 --> 00:13:28.900
You'd go out and buy a plane ticket.

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:31.540
Congress forced the territory to raise those rates

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:33.740
to keep the medical professionals on the island.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:34.860
OK, that makes sense.

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:37.740
It's essentially a federally mandated retention bonus.

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:38.420
It's exactly.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:39.580
What was the next hurdle?

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:41.580
Program integrity.

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:44.620
To unlock another $75 million,

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:47.940
Puerto Rico had to designate specific high-level officials

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:50.940
responsible for program integrity and procurement oversight.

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:52.140
Like named individuals.

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:52.940
Yes.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:55.460
Congress wanted named individuals they could hold accountable

00:13:55.460 --> 00:13:58.020
to ensure the massive health care contracts being handed out

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:01.060
to private insurance companies were clean, transparent,

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:03.220
and actively monitored for fraud.

00:14:03.220 --> 00:14:05.740
And then we get to the most technologically

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:10.020
and honestly bureaucratically complex requirement.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:12.020
The asset verification penalty.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:13.500
Oh, this one is wild.

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:17.100
This one fascinated me because of how hard it is to actually execute.

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:18.780
It was a massive mandate.

00:14:18.780 --> 00:14:22.140
Congress required that by January 1st, 2026,

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:25.820
Puerto Rico had to implement a fully electronic asset verification

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:27.860
system for Medicaid applicants.

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:30.140
Let's explain what that actually means on the ground.

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:31.940
When someone applies for Medicaid,

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:34.340
they have to prove they fall below a certain income

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:35.660
and asset threshold, right?

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:36.460
Correct.

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:38.500
Historically, this meant an applicant brought in

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:40.860
physical bank statements and case workers

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:42.220
manually verified them.

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:43.260
Lots of paperwork.

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:47.340
It was slow, prone to human error, and vulnerable to fraud.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:51.140
An electronic asset verification system, or AVS,

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:53.500
requires building an IT infrastructure that

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:56.900
uses API's application programming interfaces

00:14:56.900 --> 00:15:00.580
to directly and instantly paying financial institutions

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:04.260
to verify the applicant's bank balances in real time.

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:06.540
Building a secure government database

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:10.820
that can seamlessly talk to every major bank on the island

00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:13.020
is a colossal IT project.

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:13.900
It is.

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:16.380
And the penalty for failing to do it was brutal.

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:18.300
It was a compounding financial punishment.

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:18.500
Right.

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:21.540
If they failed to have the system operational by the deadline,

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:23.940
their FMAP rate would be incrementally reduced

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:24.820
every single quarter.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:25.300
Wow.

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:27.060
They would literally bleed matching funds

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:28.220
until they fixed it.

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:29.660
So you have a territory recovering

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:31.380
from natural disasters struggling

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:34.700
with basic grid infrastructure, and Congress tells them,

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:37.020
build a state-of-the-art financial API network

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:39.060
or we start slashing your health care budget.

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:39.980
Basically, yeah.

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:41.420
As a listener, you might expect

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:43.340
this is where the system completely fell apart.

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:45.940
But according to the internal reports we were reviewing,

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:47.140
they actually pulled it off.

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:49.740
That is one of the most remarkable parts of this story.

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:52.420
They successfully built and launched the system.

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:53.140
Incredible.

00:15:53.140 --> 00:15:55.660
The federal centers for Medicare and Medicaid services,

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:59.700
CMS, officially approved their AVS implementation

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:02.180
in June, 2026.

00:16:02.180 --> 00:16:04.180
They dodged the penalty entirely.

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:04.660
Wow.

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:08.620
They successfully implemented the 75% physician payment floor.

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:10.460
They installed the integrity leads.

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:13.020
They jumped through every single hoop.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:15.620
And there is one specific metric in these documents

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:16.980
that absolutely blew my mind.

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:18.700
It's the PRM rate.

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:18.980
Yes.

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:20.980
The payment error rate measurement.

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:22.300
This is the federal government's audit

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:25.540
of how many improper payments a Medicaid system makes,

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:29.340
whether through fraud, waste, or just clerical errors.

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:32.020
It is the gold standard metric for congressional oversight

00:16:32.020 --> 00:16:34.820
committees assessing how well a state or territory

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:36.060
is managing federal funds.

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:36.340
Right.

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:39.140
So the national average for improper Medicaid payments

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:41.900
across the 50 states is 5.09%.

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:42.260
Wow.

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:45.100
Puerto Rico's rate, 2.51%.

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:45.820
It's amazing.

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:48.140
They are running a system with less than half

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:50.220
the error rate of the national average,

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:53.740
all while operating under a constantly looming fiscal cliff.

00:16:53.740 --> 00:16:54.940
How do they achieve that?

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:57.820
When you are operating on a capped gift card,

00:16:57.820 --> 00:17:00.660
you literally cannot afford a single mistake.

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:01.220
Right.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:03.100
In an open-ended state system,

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:06.460
an improper payment is bad, but it doesn't necessarily

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:09.060
bankrupt the state because the federal funds keep flowing.

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:10.220
But in Puerto Rico,

00:17:10.220 --> 00:17:13.660
every wasted dollar is a dollar stolen from the cap.

00:17:13.660 --> 00:17:17.100
It forces hyper-conservative, highly scrutinized

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:18.500
payout mechanisms.

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:21.300
This compliance record feels like a massive

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:23.300
strategic weapon for them right now.

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:25.180
It is their entire argument.

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:27.700
When they go to Congress in 2027,

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:30.980
they aren't just begging for money based on need.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:32.780
They are walking into committee rooms

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:35.420
with Allison Mitchell's CRS reports

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:37.260
validating their compliance.

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:37.700
Yeah.

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:39.500
They can look lawmakers in the eye and say,

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:41.860
you give us the strictest conditions in the country

00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:44.780
and we outperform the national average on financial integrity,

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:46.500
now give us a permanent system.

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:48.020
That makes total logical sense.

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:49.980
You prove you can handle the responsibility,

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:51.260
you get the permanent fix.

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:52.020
Good think.

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:53.700
But this is Washington, D.C.,

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:56.180
where logic often goes to die.

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:58.620
Left pivot to a part of the legislative history

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:00.820
that frankly feels a bit maddening

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:01.900
when you first read it.

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:04.420
We need to talk about the geographic discrepancies.

00:18:04.420 --> 00:18:05.900
Ah, yes.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:08.460
The tale of the five territories.

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:10.580
This is where the politics of federal funding

00:18:10.580 --> 00:18:13.060
really clash with the realities of health care need.

00:18:13.060 --> 00:18:17.500
So when Congress passed that massive 2022 package,

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:20.420
we know they gave Puerto Rico a temporary patch,

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:24.620
bumping their FMAP up to 76% until 2027.

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:25.300
Right.

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:27.580
But Puerto Rico wasn't the only territory

00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:28.940
in that legislation, was it?

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:29.580
No.

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:32.500
The legislation also covered the U.S. Virgin Islands,

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:35.260
Guam, American Samoa, and the Commonwealth

00:18:35.260 --> 00:18:36.700
of the Northern Mariana Islands.

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:38.780
And what did Congress give those four territories

00:18:38.780 --> 00:18:40.180
in that exact same bill?

00:18:40.180 --> 00:18:42.260
Congress granted all four of those territories

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:44.460
a permanent FMAP of 83%.

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:46.540
Wait, I want to make sure I'm understanding this correctly.

00:18:46.540 --> 00:18:47.180
I know.

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:49.100
Puerto Rico, which has the highest poverty rate

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:51.020
and the largest vulnerable population,

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:54.260
gets a temporary match of 76% that threatens to expire

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:55.380
and bankrupt them.

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:58.020
But the U.S. Virgin Islands right next door

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:02.660
gets the maximum 83% match written into law permanently.

00:19:02.660 --> 00:19:06.340
How on earth does a lawmaker justify that discrepancy?

00:19:06.340 --> 00:19:08.060
Well, if you look at it purely through the lens

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:11.500
of health care equity, it is entirely unjustifiable.

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:13.940
But if you look at it through the lens of the Congressional

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:18.140
Budget Office, the CBO, it makes complete brutal sense.

00:19:18.140 --> 00:19:20.180
We have to understand how legislation is actually

00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:21.380
scored in Washington.

00:19:21.380 --> 00:19:23.380
OK, explain this scoring process,

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:25.020
because I think a lot of people here

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:28.100
see the CBO score on the news, but don't understand

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:31.300
the mechanics of how it literally kills bills.

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:34.620
The CBO acts as the official accountant for Congress.

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:36.860
Whenever a piece of legislation is proposed,

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:38.860
the CBO calculates exactly how much

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:41.500
it will cost the federal government over a 10-year window.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:42.660
A 10-year window, OK.

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:45.100
Congress operates under strict budgetary rules,

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:47.940
often called pay as you go or PY go.

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:50.020
If a bill is projected to add massive costs

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:52.060
to the federal deficit, lawmakers either

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:53.860
have to raise taxes to pay for it,

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:56.220
cut funding from somewhere else, or find the votes

00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:57.900
to bypass their own budgetary rules,

00:19:57.900 --> 00:19:59.300
which is incredibly difficult.

00:19:59.300 --> 00:20:02.220
So it all comes down to the raw price tag over 10 years.

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:03.180
Exactly.

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:04.780
Now let's look at the populations.

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:08.780
The US Virgin Islands has roughly 100,000 residents total.

00:20:08.780 --> 00:20:11.140
American Samoa has less than 50,000.

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:12.380
Very small.

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:15.580
When the CBO scores the cost of giving those territories

00:20:15.580 --> 00:20:18.380
a permanent 83% open-ended match,

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:20.620
the total dollar amount over 10 years

00:20:20.620 --> 00:20:21.860
is relatively minuscule.

00:20:21.860 --> 00:20:22.860
It's a rounding error.

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:25.500
It's essentially a rounding error in a multi-trillion dollar

00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:26.740
federal budget.

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:29.020
Wallmakers can easily absorb that cost

00:20:29.020 --> 00:20:30.940
in a massive omnibus spending bill

00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:32.140
without triggering alarms.

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:35.220
But Puerto Rico has 1.3 million beneficiaries

00:20:35.220 --> 00:20:36.620
on Medicaid alone.

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:37.460
Right.

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:39.500
If the CBO scores a bill that gives Puerto Rico

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:42.780
a permanent 83% match, the 10-year cost

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:45.860
explodes into the tens of billions of dollars.

00:20:45.860 --> 00:20:47.180
Suddenly it's not a rounding error.

00:20:47.180 --> 00:20:48.620
It's a massive budget item that

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:51.500
requires offsetting cuts or new revenues.

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:53.380
And during a lame duck congressional session,

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:56.140
which is when the 2022 patch was negotiated,

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:58.340
finding offsets for tens of billions of dollars

00:20:58.340 --> 00:21:00.380
is just politically impossible.

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:02.300
So the lawmakers compromised.

00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:04.820
They gave the small territories the permanent maximum rate

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:05.900
because it was cheap.

00:21:05.900 --> 00:21:09.260
And they forced Puerto Rico to take a lower, temporary rate

00:21:09.260 --> 00:21:11.500
because it was the only way to get the math to fit

00:21:11.500 --> 00:21:13.180
inside the political window they had.

00:21:13.180 --> 00:21:14.900
That is exactly what happened.

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:16.900
It wasn't malice against Puerto Rico.

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:19.620
It was the mechanical reality of congressional budget

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:20.420
scoring.

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:23.300
The smaller territory slipped through the budgetary cracks

00:21:23.300 --> 00:21:25.380
while Puerto Rico's sheer size made them

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:27.020
a target for fiscal hawks.

00:21:27.020 --> 00:21:29.100
But out in the real world, past the spreadsheets

00:21:29.100 --> 00:21:31.300
and the CBO scores, this disparity

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:33.260
has devastating consequences.

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:33.900
Absolutely.

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:35.540
It isn't just a political oddity.

00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:38.780
It directly impacts human lives and drives a phenomenon

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:40.700
that the sources highlight repeatedly.

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:42.580
Let's look at the actual human impact

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:44.980
of this structural underfunding.

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:47.300
The demographic data provided by the Puerto Rico

00:21:47.300 --> 00:21:50.540
Federal Affairs Administration, or PRFAA,

00:21:50.540 --> 00:21:52.980
paints a very sobering picture.

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:55.380
Puerto Rico is not just a large population.

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:57.580
It is a highly vulnerable one.

00:21:57.580 --> 00:22:00.780
Between 60% to 70% of the island's population

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:04.140
has at least one critical chronic health condition.

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:06.420
We're talking about high rates of diabetes,

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:07.980
hypertension, and asthma.

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:09.900
You have a population that desperately needs

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:12.820
robust, uninterrupted preventative care.

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:14.620
And they are relying on a system

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:16.580
built on temporary gift cards.

00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:17.340
Yes.

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:18.860
And importantly, the sources point out

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:22.140
that this funding crisis isn't limited to just Medicaid.

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:22.460
No.

00:22:22.460 --> 00:22:24.180
It bleeds heavily into Medicare as well,

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:26.180
specifically Medicare Advantage.

00:22:26.180 --> 00:22:28.140
This is a crucial parallel track.

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:28.940
OK.

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:32.540
Over 670,000 seniors in Puerto Rico

00:22:32.540 --> 00:22:35.340
rely on Medicare Advantage for their health care.

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:37.740
These are American citizens who paid Medicare taxes

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:39.300
their entire working lives.

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:41.180
And how does the funding for Medicare Advantage

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:43.300
in Puerto Rico compare to the mainland?

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:44.820
The base rates the federal government

00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:46.900
pays to Medicare Advantage plans in Puerto Rico

00:22:46.900 --> 00:22:49.740
are roughly 41% below the national average.

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:52.940
41% less for the exact same federal program.

00:22:52.940 --> 00:22:53.740
Why?

00:22:53.740 --> 00:22:55.500
Is there a different cap for Medicare?

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:57.260
The mechanism is different.

00:22:57.260 --> 00:22:59.860
But the root cause is connected.

00:22:59.860 --> 00:23:01.460
Medicare Advantage benchmarks,

00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:03.860
the rates the government pays the private plans,

00:23:03.860 --> 00:23:06.580
are heavily influenced by the historic costs

00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:09.140
of traditional fee-for-service Medicare

00:23:09.140 --> 00:23:11.020
in that specific geographic area.

00:23:11.020 --> 00:23:12.660
So because they spent less in the past,

00:23:12.660 --> 00:23:13.900
they get less now?

00:23:13.900 --> 00:23:14.980
Exactly.

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:16.540
Because Puerto Rico's health care system

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:18.100
has been structurally underfunded

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:19.900
across the board for decades,

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:21.780
the historical fees paid for services

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:23.740
are artificially low.

00:23:23.740 --> 00:23:25.260
A doctor visit in San Juan

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:27.980
simply costs less on paper than one in Miami,

00:23:27.980 --> 00:23:30.260
because the system couldn't afford to pay more.

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:31.500
It's a vicious cycle.

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:33.420
Because they were underfunded in the past,

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:35.580
their historical costs look low.

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:37.700
And because their historical costs look low,

00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:39.500
the federal government uses that data

00:23:39.500 --> 00:23:41.700
to justify underfunding them in the present.

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:42.500
Exactly.

00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:42.820
Yeah.

00:23:42.860 --> 00:23:44.140
And the geographic disparity

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:46.140
slaps them in the face here too.

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:48.140
The Medicare Advantage funding in Puerto Rico

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:50.420
is roughly 20% below the funding levels

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:52.380
in the neighboring U.S. Virgin Islands.

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:53.220
Unbelievable.

00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:55.740
Again, a discrepancy driven purely by location,

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:57.500
not by the needs of the seniors.

00:23:57.500 --> 00:23:59.580
Which leads to the most painful statistic

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:01.340
in all of these documents.

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:02.740
When you chronically starve

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:04.140
a health care system of funding,

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:05.820
when you pay doctors less,

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:08.060
when you can't upgrade hospital equipment

00:24:08.060 --> 00:24:09.340
because you don't know if your budget

00:24:09.340 --> 00:24:11.700
will exist next year,

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:13.420
what do the patients do?

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:14.420
They leave.

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:17.300
The phenomenon is known as health care emigration.

00:24:17.300 --> 00:24:18.820
And the numbers are stark.

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:20.500
According to the internal data,

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:23.100
33% of all Puerto Ricans

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:25.420
who emigrate to the mainland United States

00:24:25.420 --> 00:24:28.300
do so specifically seeking better health services.

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:29.380
33%.

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:31.900
One out of every three people packing up their lives

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:33.420
and leaving the island is doing it

00:24:33.420 --> 00:24:35.660
simply because they cannot get the medical care

00:24:35.660 --> 00:24:36.860
they need to survive.

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:38.980
Think about the sociological impact of that.

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:40.700
Families are fractured.

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:43.460
The elderly who need familial support the most

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:45.700
are forced to move thousands of miles away

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:48.100
to Florida, New York, or Texas,

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:51.940
simply to access a functioning health care safety net.

00:24:51.940 --> 00:24:53.420
It hauls out the community.

00:24:53.420 --> 00:24:54.940
But here is where the story shifts

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:57.540
from tragic to absolutely infuriating

00:24:57.540 --> 00:24:58.620
from a taxpayer perspective.

00:24:58.620 --> 00:24:59.780
Well, the financial paradox.

00:24:59.780 --> 00:25:02.340
Yes, I wanna dive into the financial paradox

00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:05.940
of this emigration because the math the PRFAA presents

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:07.140
is mind-boggling.

00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:10.500
It is the ultimate example of a broken federal policy.

00:25:10.540 --> 00:25:11.340
Let's look at the numbers.

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:13.380
When a Puerto Rican resident realizes

00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:16.660
their local clinic can't treat their chronic condition,

00:25:16.660 --> 00:25:18.020
they move to the mainland.

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:18.860
Right.

00:25:18.860 --> 00:25:21.260
The moment they establish residency in Florida

00:25:21.260 --> 00:25:24.020
or Pennsylvania, they are instantly absorbed

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:26.740
into that state's open-ended health care system.

00:25:26.740 --> 00:25:28.180
They get full Medicaid

00:25:28.180 --> 00:25:30.180
or standard Medicare Advantage rates.

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:31.020
Automatically.

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:31.940
According to the data,

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:34.980
caring for that specific migrated population

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:36.180
costs the federal government

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:39.740
approximately $165 million annually.

00:25:39.740 --> 00:25:43.500
Okay, $165 million spent on the mainland.

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:46.260
Now, if the federal government had just funded Puerto Rico

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:47.780
equitably in the first place,

00:25:47.780 --> 00:25:49.380
allowing those people to stay home

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:51.660
and receive care from their local doctors,

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:53.540
treating that exact same group of patients

00:25:53.540 --> 00:25:56.780
would have cost the federal government $44 million.

00:25:56.780 --> 00:25:58.020
I wanna make sure you really

00:25:58.020 --> 00:26:00.820
absorbed the absurdity of those figures.

00:26:00.820 --> 00:26:05.220
To save $34 million on the Puerto Rico budget ledger,

00:26:05.220 --> 00:26:07.900
the federal government actively incentivizes

00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:09.740
six citizens to migrate.

00:26:09.740 --> 00:26:10.580
Right.

00:26:10.580 --> 00:26:11.620
And when they migrate,

00:26:11.620 --> 00:26:15.180
the federal government ends up paying $165 million

00:26:15.180 --> 00:26:16.980
to treat them in mainland hospitals.

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:19.660
They are paying nearly four times as much money

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:21.180
for the exact same care,

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:24.260
simply because the patient changed zip codes.

00:26:24.260 --> 00:26:25.100
Yeah.

00:26:25.100 --> 00:26:27.700
It is beyond penny-wise and pound-foolish.

00:26:27.700 --> 00:26:30.620
It is institutional financial malpractice.

00:26:30.620 --> 00:26:32.860
It reveals the massive inefficiency

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:35.940
of trying to manage a unified national citizenry

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:38.900
with fractured localized funding silos.

00:26:38.900 --> 00:26:40.900
When you squeeze the balloon in Puerto Rico,

00:26:40.900 --> 00:26:41.900
the air doesn't disappear,

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:43.340
it just pops out in Florida,

00:26:43.340 --> 00:26:45.140
where everything is vastly more expensive.

00:26:45.140 --> 00:26:47.980
The federal taxpayer is actively losing money

00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:50.620
by refusing to fund Puerto Rico's healthcare system

00:26:50.620 --> 00:26:51.700
equitably. Without a doubt.

00:26:51.700 --> 00:26:54.380
So we have a system operating on a temporary gift card,

00:26:54.380 --> 00:26:56.580
chronically underfunded, hemorrhaging doctors,

00:26:56.580 --> 00:26:58.620
forcing sick people to flee the island,

00:26:58.620 --> 00:27:02.340
and costing federal taxpayers quadruple in the long run.

00:27:02.340 --> 00:27:04.380
In the middle of this absolute chaos,

00:27:04.380 --> 00:27:05.900
someone actually has to sit down,

00:27:05.900 --> 00:27:07.260
look at the balance sheets,

00:27:07.260 --> 00:27:10.180
and try to write a legal budget for the territory,

00:27:10.180 --> 00:27:11.980
which brings us to the financial oversight

00:27:11.980 --> 00:27:14.300
and management board, the FOMB.

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:16.460
The FOMB is the elephant in the room

00:27:16.460 --> 00:27:18.100
in Puerto Rican politics.

00:27:18.100 --> 00:27:18.940
Yeah.

00:27:18.940 --> 00:27:19.780
To understand their role,

00:27:19.780 --> 00:27:21.700
we have to touch briefly on PROMESSA.

00:27:21.700 --> 00:27:24.300
Right. The federal law passed in 2016.

00:27:24.300 --> 00:27:26.620
Because Puerto Rico is a territory,

00:27:26.620 --> 00:27:29.700
it couldn't declare traditional municipal bankruptcy

00:27:29.700 --> 00:27:33.620
when it amassed over $70 billion in unpayable debt.

00:27:33.620 --> 00:27:35.220
So Congress passed PROMESSA,

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:37.940
which created this unelected, federally appointed,

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:39.940
financial oversight and management board.

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:41.220
And they have a lot of power.

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:42.580
The board has supreme authority

00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:44.300
over Puerto Rico's finances.

00:27:44.300 --> 00:27:46.860
The elected local government can propose budgets,

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:48.900
but the FOMB has to certify them.

00:27:48.900 --> 00:27:51.460
If the FOMB says no, the budget doesn't pass.

00:27:51.460 --> 00:27:53.700
They are essentially the federally mandated parents

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:54.580
holding the checkbook.

00:27:54.580 --> 00:27:55.780
Yes, exactly.

00:27:55.780 --> 00:27:57.980
In June, 2026, the documents show

00:27:57.980 --> 00:28:02.740
the FOMB certified a $33.6 billion consolidated budget

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:04.900
for fiscal year 2027.

00:28:04.900 --> 00:28:07.420
And in their highly detailed certification documents,

00:28:07.420 --> 00:28:09.340
they slapped a massive warning label

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:10.700
on the healthcare system.

00:28:10.700 --> 00:28:11.700
They did.

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:14.100
They explicitly designated Medicaid

00:28:14.100 --> 00:28:16.260
as the top material fiscal risk

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:18.500
for the entire island's budget.

00:28:18.500 --> 00:28:19.860
And you can understand why,

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:22.020
given the structural mechanics we've discussed,

00:28:22.020 --> 00:28:24.140
the FOMB isn't looking at healthcare

00:28:24.140 --> 00:28:25.820
from a medical perspective.

00:28:25.820 --> 00:28:27.340
They are looking at it purely

00:28:27.340 --> 00:28:29.180
through the lens of risk exposure.

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:31.180
And the risk comes from how Medicaid

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:32.740
is actually administered on the ground.

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:34.500
Let's explain MCOs.

00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:37.300
In Puerto Rico, the government doesn't pay doctors

00:28:37.300 --> 00:28:39.380
directly for Medicaid services.

00:28:39.380 --> 00:28:42.340
They use managed care organizations, or MCOs.

00:28:42.340 --> 00:28:43.820
These are private insurance companies.

00:28:43.820 --> 00:28:44.660
Okay.

00:28:44.660 --> 00:28:47.220
The government pays a fixed monthly premium per person

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:50.260
to the MCO, and the MCO assumes the risk

00:28:50.260 --> 00:28:52.060
of paying the actual medical bills.

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:53.980
But the FOMB noted that the costs

00:28:53.980 --> 00:28:56.860
of these MCO contracts are rising aggressively.

00:28:56.860 --> 00:28:58.540
Because the underlying cost of healthcare

00:28:58.540 --> 00:28:59.740
is rising everywhere.

00:28:59.740 --> 00:29:01.060
You have general inflation,

00:29:01.060 --> 00:29:03.220
highly expensive new pharmaceutical treatments,

00:29:03.340 --> 00:29:04.940
an aging population, and the fact

00:29:04.940 --> 00:29:06.180
that Puerto Rico is being forced

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:09.100
to raise provider payment rates to stop the brain drain.

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:11.180
Right, the 75% floor we talked about.

00:29:11.180 --> 00:29:13.220
Right, all of this drives up the premiums

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:15.300
the MCOs demand from the government.

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:18.860
And here is where the FOMB starts sweating.

00:29:18.860 --> 00:29:21.180
If this were a 50-state model,

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:23.500
an increase in MCO costs would be split

00:29:23.500 --> 00:29:25.940
with the federal government through that open tab.

00:29:25.940 --> 00:29:27.500
But Puerto Rico has the gift card.

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:28.340
Right.

00:29:28.340 --> 00:29:29.860
If the MCO contracts suddenly jump

00:29:29.860 --> 00:29:32.660
by $500 million due to medical inflation,

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:34.100
the federal cap doesn't budge.

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:34.940
Exactly.

00:29:34.940 --> 00:29:36.620
The territory is legally on the hook

00:29:36.620 --> 00:29:38.980
for 100% of that overage.

00:29:38.980 --> 00:29:40.940
For a financial control board tasked

00:29:40.940 --> 00:29:42.700
with maintaining a delicately balanced

00:29:42.700 --> 00:29:44.500
post-bankruptcy budget,

00:29:44.500 --> 00:29:46.420
an uncapped liability colliding

00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:48.220
with a capped federal revenue stream

00:29:48.220 --> 00:29:49.340
is their worst nightmare.

00:29:49.340 --> 00:29:51.300
Which explains the incredibly aggressive

00:29:51.300 --> 00:29:53.580
control language the FOMB inserted

00:29:53.580 --> 00:29:56.460
into the 2027 budget resolution.

00:29:56.460 --> 00:29:57.820
They didn't just certify the numbers.

00:29:57.820 --> 00:29:59.300
They tied the government's hands.

00:29:59.300 --> 00:30:00.140
What did they do?

00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:02.140
They instituted a hard lock on the system.

00:30:02.140 --> 00:30:04.540
The FOMB mandated that any proposed changes

00:30:04.540 --> 00:30:06.820
to Medicaid benefits, any expansion of coverage,

00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:08.540
any alteration to services,

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:11.260
must be rigorously reviewed for its fiscal impact.

00:30:11.260 --> 00:30:12.100
It's okay.

00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:13.060
And it cannot be implemented

00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:14.780
until it is explicitly approved

00:30:14.780 --> 00:30:16.300
by both the local office of management

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:18.860
and budget and the oversight board itself.

00:30:18.860 --> 00:30:21.620
It's like a parent putting a hard spend alert freeze

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:23.500
on a teenager's debit card

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:26.140
because there's absolutely no overdraft protection.

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:26.980
Exactly.

00:30:26.980 --> 00:30:28.780
If you try to swipe for a new benefit,

00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:30.140
the transaction is denied

00:30:30.140 --> 00:30:31.820
until the board reviews the receipt.

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:35.340
They are terrified that a well-meaning local politician

00:30:35.340 --> 00:30:37.180
might expand a health benefit

00:30:37.180 --> 00:30:39.300
that accidentally pushes the total cost

00:30:39.300 --> 00:30:40.860
over the federal cap,

00:30:40.860 --> 00:30:42.940
instantly blowing a catastrophic hole

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:44.740
in the certified budget.

00:30:44.740 --> 00:30:47.220
But the FOMB documents also highlight

00:30:47.220 --> 00:30:49.620
a massive vulnerability Puerto Rico has

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:52.740
when it tries to lobby Washington for a permanent fix.

00:30:52.740 --> 00:30:55.220
And this is a point that opponents in Congress

00:30:55.220 --> 00:30:56.380
use relentlessly.

00:30:56.380 --> 00:30:57.220
Yes.

00:30:57.220 --> 00:30:59.180
The FOMB explicitly notes

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:00.220
that the Puerto Rican government

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:02.580
has not delivered audited financial statements

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:04.500
since fiscal year 2022.

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:06.300
Yes, the financial reporting lag.

00:31:06.300 --> 00:31:07.940
This is the Achilles' heel

00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:09.820
of their entire lobbying effort.

00:31:09.820 --> 00:31:11.620
I need to understand why this is happening.

00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:12.980
The sources mentioned the government

00:31:12.980 --> 00:31:14.500
is slowly transitioning to something

00:31:14.500 --> 00:31:17.540
called modified accrual accounting.

00:31:17.540 --> 00:31:18.420
What does that mean?

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:20.060
And why is it taking four years

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:21.980
to figure out how much money they have?

00:31:21.980 --> 00:31:24.300
It's a massive systemic overhaul.

00:31:25.140 --> 00:31:27.340
Historically, many parts of the Puerto Rican government

00:31:27.340 --> 00:31:30.780
operated on essentially a cash basis accounting system.

00:31:30.780 --> 00:31:31.620
Okay.

00:31:31.620 --> 00:31:33.580
Cash basis is simple.

00:31:33.580 --> 00:31:36.740
You record revenue when the cash hit the bank

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:39.780
and you record an expense when the check clears.

00:31:39.780 --> 00:31:40.860
Which is how most people

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:42.300
run their personal checking accounts.

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:43.140
Right.

00:31:43.140 --> 00:31:44.580
But it is a disastrous way

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:45.980
to run a complex government

00:31:45.980 --> 00:31:48.100
because it hides long-term liabilities.

00:31:48.100 --> 00:31:48.940
No, so.

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:50.300
Well, if you owe a billion dollars

00:31:50.300 --> 00:31:52.180
in pension payouts next year

00:31:52.180 --> 00:31:55.220
or you have massive pending MCO contracts,

00:31:55.220 --> 00:31:56.860
cash accounting doesn't show that debt

00:31:56.860 --> 00:31:58.300
until the day you write the check.

00:31:58.300 --> 00:31:59.220
Oh, I see.

00:31:59.220 --> 00:32:00.420
Modified accrual accounting

00:32:00.420 --> 00:32:02.660
is the standard for modern governments.

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:04.380
It requires recognizing revenues

00:32:04.380 --> 00:32:06.140
when they become available and measurable

00:32:06.140 --> 00:32:08.420
and crucially recording expenditures

00:32:08.420 --> 00:32:10.100
when the legal liability is incurred,

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:12.060
not just when the cash changes hands.

00:32:12.060 --> 00:32:13.580
So they are trying to transition

00:32:13.580 --> 00:32:17.140
a massive historically fragmented government bureaucracy

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:18.460
from a simple checkbook

00:32:18.460 --> 00:32:21.740
to a highly complex predictive accounting standard.

00:32:21.740 --> 00:32:22.740
Yes.

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:25.300
It requires reconciling thousands

00:32:25.300 --> 00:32:27.060
of separate agency accounts,

00:32:27.060 --> 00:32:29.340
integrating disparate IT systems

00:32:29.340 --> 00:32:31.780
and tracking down historic liabilities

00:32:31.780 --> 00:32:33.100
that were never properly recorded.

00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:34.380
That sounds awful.

00:32:34.380 --> 00:32:36.780
It is a forensic accounting nightmare,

00:32:36.780 --> 00:32:39.140
which is why they are four years behind

00:32:39.140 --> 00:32:42.060
in producing official audited financial statements.

00:32:42.060 --> 00:32:44.660
But think about how that looks in Washington.

00:32:44.660 --> 00:32:46.500
Try to put yourself in the shoes

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:49.260
of a local official heading to Capitol Hill.

00:32:49.260 --> 00:32:51.020
Imagine walking into a bank

00:32:51.020 --> 00:32:53.060
to ask for a multi-billion-dollar

00:32:53.060 --> 00:32:55.540
permanent open-ended mortgage renewal.

00:32:55.540 --> 00:32:56.380
Right.

00:32:56.380 --> 00:32:57.220
The loan officer says,

00:32:57.220 --> 00:32:58.700
okay, let's see your tax returns

00:32:58.700 --> 00:33:00.740
to prove your financial stability.

00:33:00.740 --> 00:33:03.620
And you hand them paperwork from 2022.

00:33:03.620 --> 00:33:05.780
The loan officer would laugh you out of the building.

00:33:05.780 --> 00:33:07.420
And that is exactly what skeptical

00:33:07.420 --> 00:33:09.100
congressional committee members do.

00:33:09.100 --> 00:33:11.580
It creates a massive credibility void.

00:33:11.580 --> 00:33:14.460
It's incredibly difficult for Puerto Rico's lobbyists

00:33:14.460 --> 00:33:16.580
to stand in front of the Senate Finance Committee

00:33:16.580 --> 00:33:18.140
and demand billions of dollars

00:33:18.140 --> 00:33:19.940
in permanent open-ended funding

00:33:19.940 --> 00:33:22.500
when they cannot produce a current audited ledger

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:23.860
of their own finances.

00:33:23.860 --> 00:33:26.260
It gives every fiscal hawk in Congress

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:29.340
an easy, legitimate sounding excuse to say no,

00:33:29.340 --> 00:33:31.220
or to insist on keeping the funding

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:33.340
strictly capped and temporary.

00:33:33.340 --> 00:33:34.180
Exactly.

00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:35.740
So if we pull all this together,

00:33:35.740 --> 00:33:38.500
the level of difficulty here is astronomical.

00:33:38.500 --> 00:33:39.820
Puerto Rico is facing

00:33:39.820 --> 00:33:44.180
a multi-billion-dollar fiscal cliff in 13 months.

00:33:44.180 --> 00:33:45.700
Their living expenses are capped

00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:48.020
by an external federal board.

00:33:48.020 --> 00:33:49.900
Their neighbors just got unlimited lines

00:33:49.900 --> 00:33:52.820
of credit, and they have to negotiate their survival

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:55.140
with tax returns that are four years out of date.

00:33:55.140 --> 00:33:58.020
Which means they cannot afford a single tactical error.

00:33:58.020 --> 00:33:59.700
They literally cannot wing this.

00:33:59.700 --> 00:34:01.660
And according to the internal timelines we have,

00:34:01.660 --> 00:34:03.660
they are absolutely not winging it.

00:34:03.660 --> 00:34:06.300
Let's look at the actual playbook for 2027.

00:34:06.300 --> 00:34:08.340
This is the operational strategy

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:10.620
the local government is currently executing,

00:34:10.620 --> 00:34:13.380
which they are calling the one voice strategy.

00:34:13.380 --> 00:34:15.460
This is where we pivot from diagnosing

00:34:15.460 --> 00:34:16.820
the structural disease

00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:19.500
to analyzing the real-time political cure

00:34:19.500 --> 00:34:21.340
and the architect of this strategy

00:34:21.340 --> 00:34:23.820
is Governor Jennifer Gonzalez Cologne.

00:34:23.820 --> 00:34:25.620
Which gives them a unique advantage.

00:34:25.620 --> 00:34:27.340
She isn't just luring the ropes.

00:34:27.340 --> 00:34:28.740
As the former resident commissioner,

00:34:28.740 --> 00:34:30.500
she is the person who personally negotiated

00:34:30.500 --> 00:34:31.940
the 2022 patch.

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:33.300
Yes, she knows exactly

00:34:33.300 --> 00:34:35.260
how the congressional machinery works.

00:34:35.260 --> 00:34:37.660
And more importantly, how it breaks down.

00:34:37.660 --> 00:34:39.700
She understands that in Washington,

00:34:39.700 --> 00:34:42.460
fractional lobbying guarantees failure.

00:34:42.460 --> 00:34:44.380
If the local Puerto Rican Department of Health

00:34:44.380 --> 00:34:46.620
goes to Congress asking for one thing,

00:34:46.620 --> 00:34:48.700
the hospital association asked for another,

00:34:48.700 --> 00:34:51.660
and the local health insurance administration, AC,

00:34:51.660 --> 00:34:53.060
asks for a third.

00:34:53.060 --> 00:34:54.500
Congress just ignores them?

00:34:54.500 --> 00:34:56.740
Congress simply throws up its hands,

00:34:56.740 --> 00:34:58.900
claims there is no local consensus,

00:34:58.900 --> 00:35:00.180
and ignores all of them.

00:35:00.180 --> 00:35:03.220
So to prevent that, in February, 2026,

00:35:03.220 --> 00:35:06.340
the governor signed executive order 2026006.

00:35:06.340 --> 00:35:08.020
Right, that executive order

00:35:08.020 --> 00:35:10.900
legally established the multi-sector task force.

00:35:10.900 --> 00:35:14.140
It forced every single stakeholder to the same table.

00:35:14.140 --> 00:35:16.220
The department of MOLF, ACs,

00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:18.500
the federal affairs office, PRFAA,

00:35:18.540 --> 00:35:20.540
the hospital associations, the pharmacy boards,

00:35:20.540 --> 00:35:21.660
and the medical associations.

00:35:21.660 --> 00:35:22.700
Everyone in one room?

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:23.540
Everyone.

00:35:23.540 --> 00:35:25.220
The mandate was absolute.

00:35:25.220 --> 00:35:27.260
No one goes to Washington with a separate agenda.

00:35:27.260 --> 00:35:29.140
They hammer out their differences in San Juan,

00:35:29.140 --> 00:35:31.020
design a single unified strategy,

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:33.340
and present exactly one set of demands to Congress,

00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:34.300
one voice.

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:37.740
So what exactly is the unified demand?

00:35:37.740 --> 00:35:39.780
When they sit down with the House Energy

00:35:39.780 --> 00:35:42.220
and Commerce Committee, what is the official ask?

00:35:42.220 --> 00:35:45.180
They locked in their targets in May, 2026.

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:47.660
The official ask has three distinct pillars.

00:35:47.660 --> 00:35:49.020
Okay, let's hear them.

00:35:49.020 --> 00:35:53.780
First, a base funding amount of $4.415 billion

00:35:53.780 --> 00:35:55.740
for fiscal year 2028.

00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:56.940
That's the new cap they want.

00:35:56.940 --> 00:35:57.780
Right.

00:35:57.780 --> 00:36:01.780
Second, an automatic 5% annual growth rate baked into the law

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:03.820
to naturally adjust from medical inflation.

00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:05.260
So they don't have to come back and beg

00:36:05.260 --> 00:36:07.380
every time the cost of insulin goes up.

00:36:07.380 --> 00:36:08.260
And the third pillar.

00:36:08.260 --> 00:36:09.260
A holy grail.

00:36:09.260 --> 00:36:13.940
They are demanding the permanent 83% FMAP matching rate

00:36:13.940 --> 00:36:15.980
that the other territories already possess.

00:36:15.980 --> 00:36:16.820
Parity.

00:36:16.820 --> 00:36:18.860
No more patches, no more cliffs, parity.

00:36:18.860 --> 00:36:21.220
It's an aggressive ask, especially given the CBO

00:36:21.220 --> 00:36:22.860
scoring issues we discussed earlier.

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:23.700
Very aggressive.

00:36:23.700 --> 00:36:27.180
Achieving it requires a flawless tactical execution.

00:36:27.180 --> 00:36:29.060
And the internal task force timeline

00:36:29.060 --> 00:36:32.380
reads like a military campaign, broken down month by month

00:36:32.380 --> 00:36:34.260
over the final 13 month window.

00:36:34.260 --> 00:36:35.340
Let's walk through this calendar.

00:36:35.340 --> 00:36:37.900
Okay, phase one is the immediate baseline phase,

00:36:37.900 --> 00:36:39.500
covering the period right now,

00:36:39.500 --> 00:36:41.860
leading up through September 2026.

00:36:41.860 --> 00:36:44.460
What is the primary objective here?

00:36:44.460 --> 00:36:46.340
Securing the perimeter.

00:36:46.340 --> 00:36:48.700
Before you ask for a permanent new deal,

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:51.900
you must prove you executed the old deal perfectly.

00:36:51.900 --> 00:36:54.620
They are laser focused on confirming the certifications

00:36:54.620 --> 00:36:56.060
for the physician payment floor

00:36:56.060 --> 00:36:58.340
and the program integrity leads,

00:36:58.340 --> 00:37:02.420
ensuring they pull down that extra $375 million

00:37:02.420 --> 00:37:03.940
for FY 2026.

00:37:03.940 --> 00:37:05.380
This is also where Allison Mitchell

00:37:05.380 --> 00:37:06.420
comes back into play, right?

00:37:06.420 --> 00:37:09.340
Yes, the strategic deployment of information.

00:37:09.340 --> 00:37:10.940
They are physically hand delivering

00:37:10.940 --> 00:37:13.700
Mitchell's nonpartisan CRS reports,

00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:16.980
along with their pristine PRM error rate data

00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:19.220
and the one voice executive order

00:37:19.220 --> 00:37:21.380
directly to the key committee staff.

00:37:21.380 --> 00:37:22.220
Smart.

00:37:22.220 --> 00:37:23.740
They are establishing the baseline facts

00:37:23.740 --> 00:37:25.540
and proving their administrative competence

00:37:25.540 --> 00:37:27.340
before the political bloodbath begins.

00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:28.700
And the internal documents note

00:37:28.700 --> 00:37:31.540
a very specific tactical warning for this phase.

00:37:31.540 --> 00:37:33.300
Do not tie the Medicaid negotiation

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:35.020
to the October continuing resolution.

00:37:35.020 --> 00:37:38.020
Yes, a continuing resolution or CR

00:37:38.020 --> 00:37:39.620
is a stop cap funding measure

00:37:39.620 --> 00:37:41.740
used to prevent a government shutdown

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:43.380
when Congress fails to pass a budget.

00:37:43.380 --> 00:37:44.540
And we know how those go.

00:37:44.540 --> 00:37:47.380
The task force knows that CR debates are toxic,

00:37:47.380 --> 00:37:50.100
highly partisan and chaotic.

00:37:50.100 --> 00:37:52.260
If Puerto Rico's Medicaid fix gets lumped

00:37:52.260 --> 00:37:54.900
into a threat of a federal government shutdown,

00:37:54.900 --> 00:37:56.420
it becomes a political football.

00:37:56.420 --> 00:37:57.780
They want a standalone fix.

00:37:57.780 --> 00:37:59.620
They want to be evaluated on the merits,

00:37:59.620 --> 00:38:02.660
not used as leverage in a broader partisan war.

00:38:02.660 --> 00:38:03.500
Exactly.

00:38:03.500 --> 00:38:05.260
Which brings us to phase two,

00:38:05.260 --> 00:38:09.540
October 2026 through early January 2027,

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:11.020
the lame duck session.

00:38:11.020 --> 00:38:13.940
This is notoriously the most unpredictable window

00:38:13.940 --> 00:38:15.300
in American politics.

00:38:15.300 --> 00:38:17.340
It occurs after the November elections,

00:38:17.340 --> 00:38:18.980
but before the newly elected Congress

00:38:18.980 --> 00:38:20.500
takes office in January.

00:38:20.500 --> 00:38:23.020
You have retiring lawmakers, shifting majorities,

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:25.820
and a mad scramble to pass legacy legislation.

00:38:25.820 --> 00:38:28.700
What is Puerto Rico's goal during this chaos?

00:38:28.700 --> 00:38:29.940
They want a discussion draft

00:38:29.940 --> 00:38:31.540
of their permanent legislation circulating

00:38:31.540 --> 00:38:33.100
in committees before Thanksgiving.

00:38:33.100 --> 00:38:34.700
They're actively expanding the number

00:38:34.700 --> 00:38:37.100
of bipartisan signatures on their support letters.

00:38:37.100 --> 00:38:39.340
But crucially, their internal strategy

00:38:39.340 --> 00:38:41.740
dictates an absolute firewall.

00:38:41.740 --> 00:38:43.580
They must keep the Medicaid funding debate

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:46.540
completely separated from the perennial toxic debate

00:38:46.540 --> 00:38:48.300
over Puerto Rico's political status,

00:38:48.300 --> 00:38:50.340
statehood versus independence.

00:38:50.340 --> 00:38:53.060
Because if the conversation shifts to statehood,

00:38:53.060 --> 00:38:55.580
the Medicaid funding instantly dies in committee.

00:38:55.580 --> 00:38:56.420
Exactly.

00:38:56.420 --> 00:38:57.940
It becomes an ideological battle

00:38:57.940 --> 00:38:59.900
rather than a healthcare necessity.

00:38:59.900 --> 00:39:01.780
Their secondary goal in the lame duck

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:04.940
is attempting to negotiate a lame duck bridge.

00:39:04.940 --> 00:39:06.620
A preliminary deal secured

00:39:06.620 --> 00:39:09.460
before the new Congress resets the board entirely.

00:39:09.460 --> 00:39:12.180
And optics are heavily managed here.

00:39:12.180 --> 00:39:14.340
The plan explicitly states that the governor

00:39:14.340 --> 00:39:15.900
and the current resident commissioner

00:39:15.900 --> 00:39:17.620
will conduct joint appearances,

00:39:17.620 --> 00:39:20.220
presenting the exact same numbers.

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:21.180
Total discipline.

00:39:21.180 --> 00:39:23.180
But let's be realistic, lame duck sessions

00:39:23.180 --> 00:39:26.220
rarely produce massive, multi-billion dollar

00:39:26.220 --> 00:39:28.380
permanent entitlement reforms.

00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:31.100
Assuming it fails, we roll into phase three.

00:39:31.100 --> 00:39:34.780
January through April, 2027, the new Congress.

00:39:34.780 --> 00:39:36.140
This is the grind.

00:39:36.140 --> 00:39:37.700
The heavy legislative lifting.

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:40.140
They are strategizing for committee markups.

00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:41.900
They are prepping the local secretary of health

00:39:41.900 --> 00:39:43.820
for grueling congressional testimonies.

00:39:43.820 --> 00:39:46.060
And they are engaging in trench warfare

00:39:46.060 --> 00:39:48.020
with the congressional budget office

00:39:48.020 --> 00:39:49.660
over how the bill will be scored.

00:39:49.660 --> 00:39:51.060
And this is exactly when they detonate

00:39:51.060 --> 00:39:54.020
their most powerful piece of leverage, the FOMB.

00:39:54.020 --> 00:39:56.340
It is a brilliant, almost paradoxical

00:39:56.340 --> 00:39:57.940
use of the oversight board.

00:39:57.940 --> 00:39:59.860
The task force strategy dictates

00:39:59.860 --> 00:40:03.180
securing a formal, aggressive letter from the FOMB.

00:40:03.180 --> 00:40:04.900
What does the letter say?

00:40:04.900 --> 00:40:07.660
They want the federally appointed fiscal watchdogs

00:40:07.660 --> 00:40:09.540
to state plainly to Congress

00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:13.580
that if Puerto Rico faces a $3.5 billion Medicaid cut,

00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:16.580
the certified budgets will instantaneously collapse

00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:19.260
and the entire core mission of the promissal law will fail.

00:40:19.260 --> 00:40:20.620
It's political judo.

00:40:20.620 --> 00:40:24.460
Congress installed the FOMB to fix Puerto Rico's finances.

00:40:24.460 --> 00:40:27.580
Now Puerto Rico is using the FOMB to threaten Congress.

00:40:27.580 --> 00:40:28.780
They're essentially saying,

00:40:28.780 --> 00:40:30.300
if you don't fund our healthcare,

00:40:30.300 --> 00:40:32.540
your own hand-picked financial control board

00:40:32.540 --> 00:40:35.100
says the whole restructuring experiment is dead.

00:40:35.100 --> 00:40:37.300
It forces fiscal conservatives in Congress

00:40:37.300 --> 00:40:39.860
to choose between funding territorial healthcare

00:40:39.860 --> 00:40:42.020
or watching the historic bankruptcy restructure

00:40:42.020 --> 00:40:43.980
they engineered completely unravel.

00:40:43.980 --> 00:40:45.380
It is maximum leverage.

00:40:45.380 --> 00:40:47.780
Okay, phase four, the 11th hour,

00:40:47.780 --> 00:40:49.820
May through September, 2027.

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:51.060
If Memorial Day passes

00:40:51.060 --> 00:40:52.620
and there is no standalone bill,

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:53.820
CANIX sets in.

00:40:53.820 --> 00:40:55.420
What is the contingency plan?

00:40:55.420 --> 00:40:59.300
If permanence fails, you pivot to survival.

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:02.500
The strategy dictates abandoning the standalone bill

00:41:02.580 --> 00:41:04.620
and attempting to attach a Medicaid rider

00:41:04.620 --> 00:41:07.300
to whatever must pass federal budget vehicle

00:41:07.300 --> 00:41:10.020
is moving in September, right before the deadline.

00:41:10.020 --> 00:41:11.060
They hold their nose

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:13.020
and dive into the omnibus spending bills.

00:41:13.020 --> 00:41:15.460
What is their absolute bottom line?

00:41:15.460 --> 00:41:17.780
If they cannot get the permanent 83%,

00:41:17.780 --> 00:41:19.780
what is the minimum acceptable outcome

00:41:19.780 --> 00:41:21.340
before they walk away?

00:41:21.340 --> 00:41:22.820
According to the internal parameters,

00:41:22.820 --> 00:41:23.900
the absolute floor,

00:41:23.900 --> 00:41:25.060
the minimum acceptable rider

00:41:25.060 --> 00:41:29.380
is freezing the fiscal year 2027 funding levels,

00:41:29.380 --> 00:41:31.580
adjusting those levels strictly for medical inflation

00:41:31.620 --> 00:41:33.300
and freezing the FMAP rate

00:41:33.300 --> 00:41:35.500
at either 76% or 83%

00:41:35.500 --> 00:41:36.900
for at least two additional years.

00:41:36.900 --> 00:41:39.580
So kicking the can down the road, another patch.

00:41:39.580 --> 00:41:40.780
If they can't share the disease,

00:41:40.780 --> 00:41:41.900
they will cite tooth and nail

00:41:41.900 --> 00:41:44.500
for another temporary dose of pain medication

00:41:44.500 --> 00:41:45.940
just to avoid the cliff.

00:41:45.940 --> 00:41:47.500
Looking at this entire timeline,

00:41:47.500 --> 00:41:49.500
it is exhausting just to read.

00:41:49.500 --> 00:41:51.420
It isn't a political wish list.

00:41:51.420 --> 00:41:53.260
It is a highly coordinated,

00:41:53.260 --> 00:41:55.540
multi-layered survival mechanism.

00:41:55.540 --> 00:41:56.940
It represents the culmination

00:41:56.940 --> 00:42:00.260
of years of deeply frustrating groundwork.

00:42:00.260 --> 00:42:02.940
Alison Mitchell mapped the statutory traps.

00:42:02.940 --> 00:42:06.460
The governor wrote the blueprint for the 2022 escape hatch.

00:42:06.460 --> 00:42:09.180
And now this multi-sector task force

00:42:09.180 --> 00:42:10.900
is a discoke political apparatus

00:42:10.900 --> 00:42:13.420
designed to force a reluctant Congress to act,

00:42:13.420 --> 00:42:15.180
step by agonizing step,

00:42:15.180 --> 00:42:17.260
month by month until the clock runs out.

00:42:17.260 --> 00:42:18.220
I want to take a step back

00:42:18.220 --> 00:42:20.260
and bring all of this directly to you, the listener.

00:42:20.260 --> 00:42:23.580
We have covered an immense dense landscape today.

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:25.860
We started with the fundamental plumbing of the law,

00:42:25.860 --> 00:42:28.700
the bizarre reality of section 1108G,

00:42:28.700 --> 00:42:30.940
the closed tab versus the open tab,

00:42:30.940 --> 00:42:33.380
and a statutory formula that ignores poverty

00:42:33.380 --> 00:42:35.220
and mandates a 55% match.

00:42:35.220 --> 00:42:38.020
We explored the mechanics of the 2022 patch,

00:42:38.020 --> 00:42:40.940
the looming terror of the September 2027 cliff,

00:42:40.940 --> 00:42:42.580
and the intense bureaucratic hurdles

00:42:42.580 --> 00:42:44.940
like the API-driven asset verification systems

00:42:44.940 --> 00:42:46.460
that Puerto Rico had to clear

00:42:46.460 --> 00:42:48.780
just to prove they were worthy of temporary funding.

00:42:48.780 --> 00:42:51.460
We looked at the maddening geographic discrepancies

00:42:51.460 --> 00:42:52.940
where CBO scoring rules

00:42:52.940 --> 00:42:55.580
grant permanent stability to small territories,

00:42:55.580 --> 00:42:58.500
while leaving 1.3 million Americans in Puerto Rico

00:42:58.500 --> 00:42:59.540
and Limbo.

00:42:59.540 --> 00:43:02.140
We traced the paradox of healthcare emigration

00:43:02.140 --> 00:43:05.180
where starving a local system of $44 million

00:43:05.180 --> 00:43:08.300
forces the federal taxpayer to spend $165 million

00:43:08.300 --> 00:43:10.620
treating those same citizens on the mainland.

00:43:10.620 --> 00:43:14.060
We examined the fiscal straitjacket applied by the FOMB,

00:43:14.060 --> 00:43:16.460
the debilitating lack of audited financials

00:43:16.460 --> 00:43:18.140
due to the sluggish transition

00:43:18.140 --> 00:43:19.740
to modified or cruel accounting,

00:43:19.740 --> 00:43:23.900
and finally the meticulous 13 month one voice strategy

00:43:23.900 --> 00:43:26.100
deployed to navigate this labyrinth.

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:28.340
And as a listener, you might be asking yourself,

00:43:28.340 --> 00:43:29.580
I don't live in Puerto Rico.

00:43:29.580 --> 00:43:31.100
I don't work in healthcare finance.

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:33.100
Why does this deep dive matter to me?

00:43:33.100 --> 00:43:36.380
It matters because the situation is an absolute masterclass

00:43:36.380 --> 00:43:39.700
in how obscure deeply buried federal statutory quirks

00:43:39.700 --> 00:43:41.660
can dictate the economic survival

00:43:41.660 --> 00:43:44.020
and physical health of an entire region.

00:43:44.020 --> 00:43:45.540
It shows the incredible friction

00:43:45.540 --> 00:43:47.580
between rigid legislative formulas,

00:43:47.580 --> 00:43:50.220
bureaucratic oversight and desperate human need.

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:52.020
It demonstrates that government policy

00:43:52.020 --> 00:43:54.620
is rarely about simple morality.

00:43:54.620 --> 00:43:56.700
It is driven by CBO scores,

00:43:56.700 --> 00:43:59.580
jurisdictional disputes and the sheer momentum

00:43:59.580 --> 00:44:01.340
of broken historical precedents.

00:44:01.340 --> 00:44:03.580
I wanna leave you with a final thought to mull over,

00:44:03.580 --> 00:44:05.340
one that transcends the spreadsheets

00:44:05.340 --> 00:44:07.100
and the committee hearings.

00:44:07.100 --> 00:44:10.660
We often view American citizenship as a great equalizer,

00:44:10.660 --> 00:44:13.140
but if a U.S. citizen's fundamental access

00:44:13.140 --> 00:44:14.900
to life-saving healthcare

00:44:14.900 --> 00:44:16.820
and the federal funding supporting it

00:44:16.820 --> 00:44:19.020
can fluctuate so wildly,

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:22.340
can face catastrophic multi-billion dollar cliffs

00:44:22.340 --> 00:44:24.540
simply based on whether they reside in a state

00:44:24.540 --> 00:44:26.180
versus a territory,

00:44:26.180 --> 00:44:27.740
what does that actually reveal

00:44:27.740 --> 00:44:29.540
about the true nature of federalism

00:44:29.540 --> 00:44:31.660
and equality in America today?

00:44:31.660 --> 00:44:33.660
When mere geography dictates

00:44:33.660 --> 00:44:36.100
the structural integrity of your safety net,

00:44:36.100 --> 00:44:38.820
are we truly operating under one unified nation

00:44:38.820 --> 00:44:40.540
or are we operating parallel systems

00:44:40.540 --> 00:44:42.260
where some citizens are simply deemed

00:44:42.260 --> 00:44:44.060
too expensive to fully protect?

00:44:44.060 --> 00:44:45.620
That is the structural paradox

00:44:45.620 --> 00:44:47.420
at the heart of this entire crisis.

00:44:47.420 --> 00:44:49.060
And it's a question that goes far beyond

00:44:49.060 --> 00:44:50.620
a single September deadline.

00:44:50.620 --> 00:44:51.460
Until next time.

